- PERVASIVE AUDIO NOISE root-caused + fixed (2026-07-16): render mix bus summed all audio channels at UNITY (no headroom) — mic+system-audio sum clipped softLimitSample on 34% of samples (measured). Removing the composite export shortcut had moved ALL exports onto this unstaged sum. Fix: 1/N per-channel headroom (single source unchanged/unity; multi-source guaranteed no clip, −6dB, recover via later 2-pass normalize). Proven at mix site: 16586→0 limiter hits. Single-source fidelity unregressed (THD −68dB).
- STUCK "waiting for mic" — hit AGAIN 2026-08-23 (PO: unresponsive, mic indicator still lit after
  refresh), fixed and PO-VERIFIED ("record seems to be okay"). The 2026-07-24 fix bounded each arming
  STEP; what hung was the JOINS between them (`await src.settled`, `await Promise.all(armPromises)`),
  which had no deadline — so the arming label, which only updates when a step STARTS, sat on
  "Waiting for microphone…" wherever the hang actually was. Four fixes, all additive: budgets on both
  joins (take starts with whatever is ready); a deadline on every durable-worker round trip (a worker
  that never answered hung arm(), which opens a write stream per channel); createCaptureSession takes
  an AbortSignal and the record button becomes CANCEL while arming (it used to be disabled — that is
  what "unresponsive" meant); and a pagehide guard that stops tracks, because nothing released devices
  when the page went away. Measured: cancel returns in 416 ms, pre-aborted in 1 ms, a normal take arms
  in 228 ms with zero live tracks after stop. Regression cover: `npm run exp -- armcancel`.
  WHICH step originally wedged is still unknown — the label could not say. The failure MODE is gone,
  not the specific wedge; if it recurs the arming timeline + budget warnings will name it.
- FROZEN-SOURCE TAKES (PO 2026-08-06 "switched to a tab with a game, nothing recorded, just frozen frame") — fixed +
  measured. Evidence first (real Chrome 150 / macOS 26, genuinely backgrounded tab, rAF==0): AudioWorklet tick 63/s,
  canvas.captureStream 30fps, <video>→drawImage ~56/s, MediaRecorder ~110KB/s all UNAFFECTED by hidden — the composite
  pipeline does not freeze, so a frozen file means the SOURCE stopped. Also measured: a tab under capture never goes
  hidden (Chrome force-renders it), and a static surface still yields ~1 keep-alive frame/s — so "no frame for 3s" = dead,
  not idle. Ship: src/core/capture/sourceLiveness.ts (pure, unit-tested) sampled from the composite's worklet tick →
  channel-stalled/channel-resumed events + sticky red capture banner (a toast is unseen — the user is in another tab) +
  Recording.stalled; composite marked UNUSABLE (kept running so it keeps watching; cancelled at stop) so an unedited
  export renders from the raw channels instead of copying still frames. Same treatment when a video track ENDS mid-take —
  previously readyState stayed 2, the composite repainted the last frame forever and instant export copied it (real bug).
  getDisplayMedia now hints displaySurface:'monitor' (picker opens on Entire Screen) and a tab/window pick raises a notice
  that other tabs/apps won't appear. Verified in-browser against the real module: freeze at 2.6s → stalled 5.7s, resume
  8.6s → resumed 8.7s; synthetic 4-lane take persisted stalled:[screen,camera] with composite dropped.
- CAPTURE CEILING (PO 2026-08-22 "record another Chrome tab with a 4K game → video freezes") — answers the open surface
  question above (ONE tab) and is a SECOND, different root cause: throughput, not liveness. Frames arrive in bursts rather
  than stopping dead, so the 3s stall detector never fires. A 4K surface is paid for four times on one GPU (Chrome capture
  readback + raw 8 Mbps encode + composite <video> decode & downscale 30×/s + on-screen preview decode) while the captured
  tab renders the game — and every one of those pixels is discarded at export, which is 1080p on both paths. Ship: display
  track constrained to DEFAULT_EXPORT_SETTINGS (CAPTURE_MAX_* in acquire.ts) as MAX-only constraints in the getDisplayMedia
  request + capDisplayTrack() re-apply before the channel is delivered (before its recorder exists; bounded 1.5s, failure
  tolerant). frameRate max 30, not just ideal — a 60fps game tab otherwise doubles the encode for nothing. MEASURED (real
  Chrome, hidden tab, AudioWorklet rig mirroring liveComposite, 2 runs order-reversed, idle machine): 4K delivered 21.5/11.9
  fps vs a 30 target, drawImage 13.94/8.55ms of a 33ms budget, composite encoder starved to 289/106 KB/s — same rig at 1080p
  delivered 29.8–29.9 fps, drawImage 0.97–1.04ms, encoders 597–614 KB/s. Also: per-source delivered fps logged every 10s
  from the composite tick (console only — a static screen legitimately delivers ~1 keep-alive fps). STILL NEEDS: PO recheck
  on the real 4K game tab, plus the new `[capture] display capped …` / `delivering N fps` console lines from that take.
